        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 开花",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "开花 — kāihuā — \"to bloom; to flower\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "这些花三月开花 zhèxiē huā sānyuè kāihuā — These flowers come out in March."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 花 (huā) — seven strokes: the grass piece of 菜 on top, then the person piece, then two strokes like the right half of 比. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "开花 (kāihuā) is built from 开 'to open' and 花 (huā) 'flower', both inherited from Middle Chinese, khoj and xwae (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which notes the second as the everyday form of the word for blossom."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for describing things."
